of reminded to Liliana Bodoc, <i>The Days of the Deer</i> - it's an epic fantasy inspired on the colonization of America. Tho it's very different from Mage or real life, it has the &quot;spirit&quot; of trying to found an alliance of different yet similar beliefs against a foreign power that want's subjugation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-28T15:51:21-07:00","id":1559075,"title":"","content":"Witches and Sorcerers were often politically powerful even in our reality were magic doesn't seem to work. As many African kings sold inconvenient relatives into slavery to stabilize their thrones I could see kings and other power brokers doing the same to ritual specialists who became powerful enough to threaten thrones. Even in Gaiman's <b>American Gods</b> the story of the twins is a good example of how it could happen. The Uncle's motivations in the novel are a sound historical portrait of how either nobles or ritual specialists could get sold into slavery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-04T13:39:43-07:00","id":1559219,"title":"","content":"I'm wondering who among the tribal warlords thought to themselves <i>&quot;hey, these people have supernatural powers that we barely comprehend. Let